%I A020139 #30 Oct 31 2026 03:29:27
%S A020139 10,15,70,133,190,259,305,481,645,703,793,1105,1330,1729,2047,2257,
%T A020139 2465,2821,4577,4921,5041,5185,6601,7869,8113,8170,8695,8911,9730,
%U A020139 10585,12403,13333,14521,14981,15841,16705,17711,18705,23377,24130,24727,26335,26467
%N A020139 Pseudoprimes to base 11.
%C A020139 According to _Karsten Meyer_, May 16 2006, 10 should be excluded, following the strict definition in Crandall and Pomerance.
%C A020139 Composite numbers k such that 11^(k-1) == 1 (mod k).
%D A020139 Richard Crandall and Carl Pomerance, Prime Numbers - A Computational Perspective, Second Edition, Springer Verlag, 2005, ISBN 0-387-25282-7, Page 132 (Theorem 3.4.2. and Algorithm 3.4.3).
%D A020139 Jean-Marie De Koninck, Ces nombres qui nous fascinent, Entry 190, p. 57, Ellipses, Paris, 2008.
%H A020139 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A020139/b020139.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (terms 1..726 from R. J. Mathar, terms 727..1000 from T. D. Noe)
%H A020139 Fred Richman, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20230727074841/https://www.math.fau.edu/Richman/carm.htm">Primality testing with Fermat's little theorem</a>. [Wayback Machine link]
%H A020139 <a href="/index/Ps#pseudoprimes">Index entries for sequences related to pseudoprimes</a>.
%t A020139 base = 11; t = {}; n = 1; While[Length[t] < 100, n++; If[! PrimeQ[n] && PowerMod[base, n-1, n] == 1, AppendTo[t, n]]]; t (* _T. D. Noe_, Feb 21 2012 *)
%o A020139 (PARI) isok(k) = k > 1 && Mod(11, k)^(k-1) == 1 && !isprime(k); \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 31 2026
%Y A020139 Cf. A001567 (pseudoprimes to base 2).
%K A020139 nonn,changed
%O A020139 1,1
%A A020139 _David W. Wilson_
